=====================================================================
(3) THE WARM-CACHE CLAIM -- **FAIL**, both the reason and the claim
=====================================================================
The sentence under test, warnings_client_regression_test.go:35-38:
  "... `make check`, `go test -short`, even a warm test cache (this test execs
   node and reads a file outside its package directory, so the cache never
   replays it) -- fails through this test's own t.Fatal ..."

3a. THE STATED REASON IS WRONG. Primary source, this machine's toolchain,
$(go env GOROOT)/src/cmd/go/internal/test/test.go, computeTestInputsID():
  case "open":
      if a.Package.Root == "" || search.InDir(name, a.Package.Root) == "" {
          // Do not recheck files outside the module, GOPATH, or GOROOT root.
          break
      }
      fh, err := hashOpen(name)                     (lines ~2049-2062)
Three things follow, and all three contradict the comment:
  - The boundary is the MODULE ROOT (`a.Package.Root`), not "its package
    directory". accounts.html and testdata/js/warnings_dom_harness.js are both
    INSIDE the module root, so they are hashed and rechecked -- a good property,
    but the opposite of the one claimed.
  - A read outside that boundary does not disable caching; the `break` means it
    is simply NOT RECHECKED. Reading a far-away file makes the cache LESS
    conservative, never more.
  - "execs node" has no bearing at all: exec is not one of the testlog opcodes
    (the switch handles only getenv/chdir/stat/open; anything else is a parse
    error, and exec produces no entry). Sub-processes are invisible to the
    cache.
The mechanism that actually invalidates is `getenv PATH`, recorded by
exec.LookPath and hashed by `case "getenv": fmt.Fprintf(h, "env %s %x\n", name,
hashGetenv(name))`. Demonstrated directly -- same node, only the PATH STRING
changed by appending an empty directory:
  $ PATH="$nodedir:$STRIPPED"           go test ./internal/handlers/accounts/  -> (cached)
  $ PATH="$nodedir:$STRIPPED:$harmless" go test ./internal/handlers/accounts/  -> 0.224s  [MISS, re-ran]
  $ PATH="$nodedir:$STRIPPED:$harmless" go test ./internal/handlers/accounts/  -> (cached)
The PATH string is the key input. The two properties the comment names are not.
So the attempt-6 worker's own verification note (PATH in the cache key) is the
correct account, and the comment states a different one. `checker-second`'s
attribution to exec+outside-package-dir is also incorrect.

3b. THE CLAIM ITSELF IS ALSO FALSE. "the cache never replays it":
  $ go test ./internal/handlers/accounts/      -> ok ... 0.203s
  $ go test ./internal/handlers/accounts/      -> ok ... (cached)
  $ go test ./internal/handlers/accounts/      -> ok ... (cached)
It replays it. And the enclosing claim -- that a warm cache still "fails through
this test's own t.Fatal" -- is false in the scenario that matters, node removed
without a PATH change (uninstalling node in place, an nvm version pruned, a base
image losing the package: PATH survives all of these):
  $ export PATH="$scratch/nodedir:$STRIPPED"   # nodedir holds a symlink to node
  $ make test                     -> exit 0,  ok budget2/internal/handlers/accounts 0.242s
  $ make test                     -> exit 0,  ok budget2/internal/handlers/accounts (cached)
  $ rm $scratch/nodedir/node ; command -v node   -> rc 1   # NO node anywhere on PATH
  $ make test                     -> exit 0,  ok budget2/internal/handlers/accounts (cached)
      grep -c 'was not found on PATH' <output>  ->  0
That is a fully green `make test`, exit 0, on a machine with no node at all.
The same sequence at the `go test ./internal/handlers/accounts/` level gives
`(cached)` and exit 0 too. The comment tells a reader the warm-cache case is
covered; it is not.
This is not a nit about phrasing. It is the same failure mode the task has now
been failed for twice: a sentence asserting reach that nobody ran a command
against. Brief item 3 asked me to rule on the reason; the reason is wrong AND
the conclusion is wrong.
For the record, the true and checkable statement is narrower: a warm cache is
invalidated when the PATH string changes (which is how node usually disappears
in practice, and why every COVERED row above really did fail), and when
accounts.html or the harness JS is edited (both inside the module root, so
hashed by case "open"); it is NOT invalidated when node vanishes at a fixed
PATH. Either say that, or say nothing about the cache and lose nothing -- the
seven named invocation paths stand on their own.

